"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.4866},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48450000000000004},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.4817},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.48},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47450000000000003},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4693},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4661},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4585},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.44799999999999995},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4457},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.4391},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4322},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.42369999999999997},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.4114},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.396},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3908}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":144,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"A British officer worshipped a Hindu goddess and one stormy night she saved his life. His name was Ross Peter. Everyday passing the Meenakshi Temple, he stepped off his horse, removed his hat and shoes and walked. Locals called him Peter Pandian. Then one stormy night, Peter woke up to the sound of anklets. A girl stood there calling his name. He followed her into the rain. Instant he stepped [music] out, his house collapsed behind him. He turned and the girl was gone. He believed goddess Meenakshi herself had saved him. [music] So he gave her a gift, a solid golden shoes with his name carved underneath. Two centuries on, the goddess still wears Peter's shoes at every festival. Just a generation later, British officials who admired local culture started despising them. And it's because of this book written by this [music] man."}}